import Dregg2.Bridge.PicklesProofChainGate
import Dregg2.Circuit.Emit.PastaPoseidonFq
/-!
# Dregg2.Bridge.MinaStateHashWordGate — the RUNTIME-CALLABLE **proof↔`stateHash`** derivation,
`@[export] dregg_mina_state_hash_word_ok`: the served block header enters a Mina Wrap
verification at exactly ONE place, and this module computes that place, per block, in
COMPILED Lean.

## What the object is, and why there is only one

A Wrap proof's `messages_for_next_step_proof.app_state` is `()` on the wire. The block enters
only as the **verifier-supplied** `app_state`, and `blockchain_snark_state.ml:384` fixes what that
is — `[| (Protocol_state.hashes t).state_hash |]`, **one `Fp` element**. It is absorbed into a
93-element Poseidon over `Fp`

```text
  word12 = Poseidon_fp( index_to_field_elements(dlog_plonk_index)   -- 56, the Wrap VK
                      ‖ [state_hash]                                --  1, THE BLOCK
                      ‖ (sg₀, chals₀ ‖ sg₁, chals₁) )               -- 36, this proof's accumulators
```

and that digest is **public-input word 12 of 40**
(`composition_types.ml:826-882`; `prepared_statement.rs:53-182`). Word 11 is the analogous
`messages_for_next_wrap_proof` digest, a 32-element Poseidon over **`Fq`** (`wrap_hack.ml:45-56`).

## MEASURED, from o1-labs' and openmina's own code — not inferred from the type

`metatheory/fixtures/pickles-extractors/src/bin/state_hash_binding_export.rs`, run 2026-07-29 over
**six real devnet blocks** (539795…539799 consecutive, plus the anchor 539508):

| observation | result |
|---|---|
| `kimchi::verifier::verify` on each block under its OWN `stateHash` | **6 / 6 `Ok`** |
| ⚑ the same proof under EVERY FOREIGN block's `stateHash` | **30 / 30 `Err`** |
| words other than 12 that move when only the `stateHash` is swapped | **0** (asserted per word, per pair) |
| our 93-element transcription vs openmina's private `to_fields` + `hash_fields` | **6 / 6 equal** |
| our 32-element `mnw` transcription vs openmina's | **6 / 6 equal** |

So the refusal is **o1-labs' verifier's**, not ours, and word 12 is the sole carrier of the header.

## ⚑ A PRIOR HYPOTHESIS THIS LANE REFUTED, and it is why the gate stops where it stops

`docs/MINA-REAL-BLOCK-GATE.md` §8.5 proposed a cheap closed loop: word 12 → the 40 words →
`public_comm` → the Fq-sponge → β, γ, α′, ζ′, *"which ARE on the wire"*. **They are not.** The same
run measured every adjacent pair of the six blocks:

  * child `statement.proof_state.deferred_values.plonk.{beta,gamma,alpha,zeta}`
    **= 0 / 5** against the parent's Wrap oracles, and
  * child `sponge_digest_before_evaluations` **= 0 / 5** against the parent's Wrap `fq_digest`.

A Wrap proof's own Fiat–Shamir challenges are absorbed into no served field, of its own block or of
its child: a Wrap statement's `deferred_values` describe the **Step** proof it wrapped, not the
previous Wrap. The only equation in `kimchi::verifier::verify` that a wrong word 12 can falsify is
therefore the **terminal IPA opening**, whose honest per-block cost includes the `2^15`-point
`⟨s, srs.g⟩` MSM (`docs/MINA-REAL-BLOCK-GATE.md` §6.1 rung 5h — the family unrooted at
`228e51de7` precisely for being unaffordable). **That is why this module derives and welds rather
than closing a challenge equation, and why no part of it may be described as a Wrap verification.**

## What IS closed here, and it was open

  * The observer never touched the `stateHash` arithmetically. Now it does: word 11 and word 12 are
    **derived** from the served header and the served proof bytes, in compiled Lean, per block.
  * ⚑ `word12`'s preimage CONTAINS the accumulator that `Dregg2.Bridge.PicklesProofChainGate`
    compares against the parent block's own `sg` — `word12_preimage_carries_the_chain_accumulator`
    proves the two modules are talking about the same 2 field elements. So word 12 jointly commits
    **the served header** and **this proof's position in the proof chain**, and the two gates
    compose instead of sitting beside one another.
  * `MinaWrapPublicInputFromHeader` (kernel, NOT rooted) welds word 12 to
    `MinaWrapPublicCommGate.PUBLIC_INPUT[12]` — the literal the whole in-kernel ladder (C3, C5, C8,
    rungs 5a–5h) is stated over. That turns "we verified a Wrap proof against 40 numbers" into
    "against the header a devnet node served".

## What is NOT closed, and must never be claimed

  * **This is not a Wrap verification and not a Mina light client.** An endpoint can still serve a
    genuine consecutive run of real proofs under fabricated headers and be refused only by the
    checks above it (the chain gate) plus the anchor weld — not by a per-block equation.
  * The 56 VK elements are **trusted config** (P8/P9 is not started), and the six wire-DROPPED
    public-input words (`combined_inner_product`, `b`, `zeta_to_srs_length`,
    `zeta_to_domain_size`, `perm`, `xi`) are **not computed here**: `expand_deferred` is rendered
    in `docs/MINA-REAL-BLOCK-GATE.md` §9 from both implementations, and is not in this gate.

## Compiled, not kernel

Every real-data pin below is a `#guard` — the COMPILED evaluator, milliseconds — not `by decide`.
A 93-element Poseidon under the kernel is the 3.5-hour wall §7 measured, for no gain: the kernel's
job is the CHECKER (§2's theorems), the differential's job is the INSTANCE (§4's pins).
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option maxRecDepth 100000

namespace Dregg2.Bridge.MinaStateHashWordGate

open Dregg2.Circuit.Emit.PastaField (pN qN)
open Dregg2.Circuit.Emit.PastaPoseidonFq (fpParams fqParams)
open Dregg2.Circuit.Emit.PastaPoseidonFq.Core (hash)

